ship and move us  forward that one night it changed  people's feelings and then of course he  sets together an emergency Banking Act  he sets together the hundred days and he  gives jobs to people and he unemployment  insurance and he treats it as a war in  the economic system began to rebound  it's an extraordinary case that we  should remember with great hope right  now I think  we keep hearing that this is  unprecedented doors can you think of  things in the past that might give her  some comfort to say listen we've been  here before where lives have been this  disrupted yeah I think that most  disruption that's in recent history  would be World War two I mean think of  what it was like for those people who  had suddenly to raishin all sorts of  items so gasoline was rationed you  couldn't drive pleasure driving anymore  in the automobile had become you know a  moment of independence only essential  war workers could get the kind of cards  that need the gasoline at the same time  food was rationed who had stamps that  allowed you to get either food or shoes  or clothing the hardest one evidently  they said was when they rationed coffee  once one cup a day because it was coming  from Latin America and the ships  couldn't be used to take it here but  they took it in stride after a while and  it prevented price gouging and it  prevented hoarding but the most  important thing was Roosevelt understood  that he had to talk to people about why  we were sacrificing and what the end  result would be and they did it then in  good spirit and he even called for  rubber drives when we were in shortage  of rubber asking people bring in old  rubber girdles bring in old rubber toys  forty tons of rubber were brought to  village greens and it meant that the  citizens felt they were part of it which  is such an important important motion in  this thing right now if we're going to  get through this together  Doris a crisis like this can really come  to define a presidency candidate without  a question I mean think about the  leaders who are the top in historical  rankings George Washington Abraham  Lincoln and Franklin Roosevelt each one  faced a crisis of the revolution the  Civil War World War two and the  depression and each one used their  leadership qualities their own personal  qualities of empathy and understanding  and moving forward and acknowledging  mistakes and experimenting and  communicating in a way that was  trustworthy and they're remembered as  our best presidents but at the same time  a crisis can allow you to fail Buchanan  who made things worse in the 1850s is  considered probably the lowest among our  presidential leaders and Herbert Herbert  Hoover was unable to use the federal  government's resources to deal with the  depression until the very end so it was  left to the states and the localities  and it simply wasn't enough so he's now  near the bottom  the list of the presidents so gives you  opportunity for greatness but also for  failure oh do you see a silver lining  quickly doors here I do I mean I think  if we look back at history and we  remember that the people who lived in  those early days of the civil war or the  Great Depression they did not know how  it was going to end there was an  enormous anxiety and yet history tells  us that each one of those situations the  leaders came through the citizens came  through and we really did emerge  stronger than ever before history  provides solace and perspective it's why  I love it so much I think we'd go back  and remember these things yeah even  that's that's important it's comforting  to see you Doris Kearns thank you so  much for joining us Doris Kearns Goodwin
 

